## I want an excuse to use it

Why am I still circling it? Partly because it [rhymes with Miriam Suzanne's argument about combining CSS units](/posts/20250927-the-best-css-unit-might-be-a-combination/): the platform usually has a primitive already, and the trick is recognising it. Variable fonts are quietly turning into a fairly expressive runtime, and Datatype is one of the more playful demonstrations of that.

And partly because it's a nice thing to look at an old idea of your own and see someone else carry the same name down the opposite road, and do it well.

If I had to put a number on it: my enthusiasm for variable fonts as an expressive runtime is sitting at about <span class="chart" aria-hidden="true">{p:85}</span><span class="kh-u-sr-only">(roughly 85 percent)</span>, while my likelihood of actually reaching for Datatype on the next chart I ship is somewhere around <span class="chart" aria-hidden="true">{p:25}</span><span class="kh-u-sr-only">(roughly 25 percent)</span>. The gap between those two pies is basically the whole post.

